[{:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-331276"] :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}],\\,x(t)={e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem and its solution given above. What is the tangent vector to the solution curve at the point corresponding to $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the *phase plane* of the system. A particular solution of an initial value problem is called a *trajectory* (or *orbit*).\n\nFirst, we compute the point that corresponds to $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$ on the given trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & ={e}^{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)}[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+{e}^{-2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)}[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{59}{4} \\\\ \\frac{11}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation of our system, we obtain the tangent vector to the trajectory.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{59}{4} \\\\ \\frac{11}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2(\\frac{59}{4})-2(\\frac{11}{2}) \\\\ 2(\\frac{59}{4})-3(\\frac{11}{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{37}{2} \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186164155 :answer-field/correct 17592186164159 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186164160 :answer-field/correct 17592186164163 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-331282"] :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t)-2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)+3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. Find equations of the $x$- and $y$-nullclines for the corresponding phase space.$\\,$\nThe $x$-nullcline is $y=$ {{field-1}}. \n\n$\\,$\nThe $y$-nullcline is $y=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Recall that: \n\n- The $x$-nullcline is a line for which ${x}^{′}=0$ (i.e., the tangents to trajectories are vertical). So, to find it, we set the first equation of the system equal to $0:$\n\n$$\n2x-2y=0\\,⇒\\,y=x\n$$\n\n- The $y$-nullcline is a line for which ${y}^{′}=0$ (i.e., the tangents to trajectories are horizontal). So, to find it, we set the second equation of the system equal to $0:$\n\n$$\n2x+3y=0\\,⇒\\,y=-\\frac{2}{3}x\n$$\n\nPlotting these in the phase space, we obtain the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073694/assets/6ba5066fd4ddc43491c3d007355e0b64c84d449d6462812aa4c8ca0450887a8b.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-332627/selection/3091d79458b2a828a3e0ca59b5e2872a84a67d49fe642d51f40a1f5b9f832a4f" :answer/id #uuid "6958974a-cb9b-5415-81c1-e753d1d2c88c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332627/selection/0c976c9f33f0e1677fca1fa4e080aba4075846445a0e807ede588f2d6145d87e" :answer/id #uuid "9f8be66a-630c-5c07-b669-698561814a26" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}14 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332627/selection/eaba9880b466d7bf13d9ba70cc12db9a3f13c74f7469196f9adf967fb0613b7e" :answer/id #uuid "810b9991-6bbc-578d-a5a2-932f87988609" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ 18\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332627/selection/b59447fbd6c1b9c0d8b67a0548ba50003a0a9f62990042783934bcd56e69b206" :answer/id #uuid "0d218137-14ef-59f8-ac5d-cf6181c3ea36" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332627/selection/763bd58eebe3595e835f7dcd3707774e8e2926fdfaf87b67a6e5f307a0d01b10" :answer/id #uuid "f09121ea-3b99-556c-917c-3479af3d2ed3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332627/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "6ba321af-28d5-5d6d-a0f1-8e3ed39a4281" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-332627/selection/eaba9880b466d7bf13d9ba70cc12db9a3f13c74f7469196f9adf967fb0613b7e" :answer-field/choices ["q-332627/selection/3091d79458b2a828a3e0ca59b5e2872a84a67d49fe642d51f40a1f5b9f832a4f" "q-332627/selection/0c976c9f33f0e1677fca1fa4e080aba4075846445a0e807ede588f2d6145d87e" "q-332627/selection/eaba9880b466d7bf13d9ba70cc12db9a3f13c74f7469196f9adf967fb0613b7e" "q-332627/selection/b59447fbd6c1b9c0d8b67a0548ba50003a0a9f62990042783934bcd56e69b206" "q-332627/selection/763bd58eebe3595e835f7dcd3707774e8e2926fdfaf87b67a6e5f307a0d01b10"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-332627" :question/id #uuid "556ea4da-7963-5f2a-ac1a-0192abc563d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-332627" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,x(t)={e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem and its solution given above. What is the tangent vector to the solution curve at the point corresponding to $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the *phase plane* of the system. A particular solution of an initial value problem is called a *trajectory* (or *orbit*).\n\nFirst, we compute the point that corresponds to $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3$ on the given trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) & ={e}^{2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =9[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation of our system, we obtain the tangent vector to the trajectory.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1(10)+1(8) \\\\ 1(10)+1(8)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ 18\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-332627/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74b8d3a6-0298-5f65-ad28-e2fc3cde731d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-332627"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-332873/selection/0fe58cc64a485e97033377700b86dab20b0ee74ac643dabafce61a43de04207a" :answer/id #uuid "07a858cd-ae80-5d80-bf6d-237251530561" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073694/assets/d49ef93ba4beb20d33346f90cab0d2b0c6cd2833b10a3390facfdf2d070e0c18.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332873/selection/767acb77ff7876efe1558062ce1ccdf15d16036f5c29a4540485e61cb245946f" :answer/id #uuid "4ec2d49d-7b85-5f91-966a-fc566b801436" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073694/assets/41a653adc61eec8ab01704b2171e14c8dd0454880db7c643929142f7544a23c6.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332873/selection/8aba8d37c2b8e